From 7ebbac5e34967b3f87acb33e6dcb23d4a0a8f4e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mminin <mminin2010@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:29:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] XFA: Show characters the standard fonts lack with system
 fonts
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Text was drawn only with the standard 14 fonts, in WinAnsiEncoding, so
every other character became a question mark: Cyrillic, Greek, Chinese
and most of Latin Extended, such as the Ł of a Polish name, but also the
bullets and arrows of 13 corpus forms, such as the "►" after "Social
insurance number" in IMM 5444.

For such characters, find a system font the way poppler does for
AcroForm fields, with GlobalParams::findSystemFontFileForUChar() and a
standard font to emulate, so that sans, serif, bold and italic text get
a similar face. Measure them with its metrics through FreeType and draw
them with a Type0 font, Identity-H encoding and a ToUnicode map, so that
text can still be copied.

Fonts are embedded with the glyphs of other characters emptied, which
keeps glyph ids while their outlines compress to almost nothing, and
without tables PDF does not use. One face is taken out of font
collections. Two Chinese characters made a 13 MB file and a 1.9 second
render with the whole font; they now take 1.6 MB, most of it the
subroutines the glyphs may call.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
